-- title:
--   Component generic points of geometric fibres lie in the finite-j chart
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a non-zero natural number $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $p$, and let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ which equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the image of the function field `x1FunctionFieldC ℚ (M * p)` under the coefficientwise embedding `coeffEmb L` of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ into $L((q))$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, equipped with an $A$-algebra structure on $L$ making $L$ its fraction field, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$; assume moreover an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \to L \to K$. Let $j \in K$ be non-zero with image in $L((q))$ equal to `coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`, the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-invariant. The assertion is: for every algebraically closed field $k$, every morphism $x \colon \operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and every irreducible component $Z$ of the underlying space of the pullback of [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) along $x$, the generic point of $Z$ belongs to the open subset obtained by pulling back, along the first projection of that pullback, the open subscheme [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartFinOpen A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L280), namely the range of the chart immersion `ιFin A K j` with source the spectrum of the subalgebra `chartAlg A K {j}` of $K$.
--
--   Here `modelTo A K j` is the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ of the two-chart integral model of $X_1(Mp)$ over the discrete valuation ring $A$, glued from the affine chart where $j$ is regular and the chart where $1/j$ is regular; the statement says that no irreducible component of a geometric fibre is contained in the complement of the finite-$j$ chart, so that each such component may be studied inside that single affine chart. It is used in the analysis of the special fibre, in particular by the results describing the minimal primes and the Gauss reduction data of the special fibre of this model.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_XOneP_genericPoint_mem_preimage_chartFinOpen_of_mem_irreducibleComponents_twoChartModel_x1_mul.lean

